(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-pharm-e2-vignettes"]={"title":"Ophthalmic Drugs — Clinical Vignettes 1","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 54-year-old has an intraocular pressure of 26 mmHg, but the optic nerve and visual fields are normal. What is this called?","choices":["Normal pressure","Ocular hypertension","Open-angle glaucoma","Angle-closure glaucoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — pressure above normal WITHOUT optic nerve damage or field loss. Normal runs about 10 to 21. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A newborn needs prophylaxis against ophthalmia neonatorum. Which agent?","choices":["Erythromycin ointment","Azithromycin solution","Moxifloxacin solution","Natamycin suspension"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is the named agent for newborn prophylaxis, and it is inexpensive. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man has used a vasoconstrictor drop for three days with no improvement in his red eye. What should he do?","choices":["Continue for another week and reassess","Double the frequency","Switch to a second vasoconstrictor","Stop the drop and see a provider"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. No improvement within 72 hours is the stated trigger for review. Because something more serious may be going on. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man has used an over-the-counter redness-relief drop every day for a month. His eyes are redder than when he started. What has happened?","choices":["He has bacterial conjunctivitis","The drops have raised his intraocular pressure","Rebound hyperaemia","He has developed allergic conjunctivitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beyond about two weeks, stopping leaves the eye redder than it started. This is the counselling point most worth carrying out of this topic. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 34-year-old has a dendritic corneal lesion consistent with herpes simplex keratitis. Which TOPICAL agent is available?","choices":["Natamycin","Trifluridine","Valacyclovir","Foscarnet"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and its ocular toxicity is punctate keratopathy. Ganciclovir is the other ophthalmic preparation. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 58-year-old with rheumatoid arthritis has chronic dry eye with inflammation. Beyond tear substitutes, which agent is indicated?","choices":["Cyclosporine drops","A topical antihistamine","A vasoconstrictor","An ophthalmic antibiotic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It inhibits interleukin 2 production, reduces lacrimal inflammatory markers and raises tear production. Ocular burning is the commonest complaint. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 55-year-old has been using tobramycin drops for five days and now has worsening pain and a corneal defect. What has happened?","choices":["Rebound hyperaemia","Corneal ulceration","Ordinary ocular irritation, common to all ophthalmic antibiotics","A white precipitate from the drug"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, along with reactive keratoconjunctivitis. It is one of the few ocular adverse effects specific enough to name a class. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 68-year-old on a prostaglandin analogue still has inadequate pressure control. What is the principle for adding a second agent?","choices":["Add a second prostaglandin analogue","Increase the prostaglandin to twice daily","Add a topical steroid","Add an agent with a DIFFERENT mechanism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Targeting a second route gives synergy, and fewer drops improves compliance. Which brand pairs which two drugs matters far less than the principle. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A parent struggles to get drops into a wriggling toddler's eye. What do you suggest, and what do you warn about?","choices":["A drop given twice as often","An oral antibiotic instead","A vasoconstrictor to reduce redness first","An ointment, which blurs vision briefly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ointments suit children and poor compliance, and the blurring means no driving until it clears. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 60-year-old with a strong family history of glaucoma needs a topical steroid for anterior uveitis. What is the particular concern, and what might you choose?","choices":["Raised intraocular pressure","Cataract formation — choose difluprednate","Corneal ulceration — choose prednisolone","Rebound hyperaemia — avoid steroids entirely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pressure elevation is more likely with a family history, and the soft steroids carry lower pressure risk. Consider a soft steroid such as loteprednol. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 22-year-old has watery, red eyes after a family member had the same. Adenoviral conjunctivitis is diagnosed. What is the treatment?","choices":["Symptomatic relief of irritation","Trifluridine drops","Oral valacyclovir","A topical fluoroquinolone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It runs a self-limited course. There is no antiviral for it. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 38-year-old reports a foreign body sensation after gardening. Which agent reveals an epithelial defect?","choices":["Tropicamide","Phenylephrine","Fluorescein","Proparacaine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it discloses corneal injury and stains the anterior segment. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 21-year-old contact lens wearer is treated for conjunctivitis. When may she resume her lenses?","choices":["When the eye is not inflamed and there has been no discharge for 24 hours","Immediately, as long as she is using drops","After finishing the full antibiotic course, regardless of the eye","After 7 days in all cases"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and she should get rid of her eye makeup too. With the lens discarded or disinfected. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 59-year-old starting cyclosporine drops should be warned about which common effect?","choices":["Ocular burning","A bitter taste","Darkening of the iris","Bradycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside foreign body sensation and blurred vision. In about one in six patients. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 47-year-old farm worker develops fungal keratitis after a corneal injury with plant material. Which commercially available ophthalmic agent treats it?","choices":["Trifluridine","Natamycin","Amphotericin B drops","Oral fluconazole alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the only commercially available ophthalmic antifungal. It covers Aspergillus, Candida, Cephalosporium, Fusarium and Penicillium. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 29-year-old presents with an eye complaint. What must be done BEFORE instilling any medication?","choices":["Stain with fluorescein","Document a visual acuity","Measure intraocular pressure","Dilate the pupil"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, along with documenting allergies and the date and result of the last eye examination. Acuity is repeated at every follow-up. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 28-year-old has itchy, watery eyes each spring and wants something that works today. Which class do you choose?","choices":["An ophthalmic glucocorticoid","An ophthalmic non-steroidal","An ophthalmic antihistamine","A mast cell stabiliser"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and they are typically preferred over mast cell stabilisers. Allow two weeks to judge full efficacy. Which works within minutes. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 62-year-old is newly diagnosed with open-angle glaucoma and has risk factors. Which class do you start?","choices":["A carbonic anhydrase inhibitor","A topical glucocorticoid","A prostaglandin analogue","A cholinergic agonist"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — first line and the most commonly used, dosed once daily. A beta blocker is the other reasonable start. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 74-year-old on timolol drops for glaucoma develops systemic side effects. By what route did the drug reach the circulation?","choices":["Transcorneal absorption into the bloodstream","Absorption through the eyelid skin","Swallowed residue from the eyelashes","Nasolacrimal drainage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. What runs down the duct is absorbed systemically without passing through the liver first. Which also avoids first-pass metabolism. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"You are teaching a 72-year-old to use eye drops. What is the MOST important step?","choices":["Holding a finger over the lacrimal duct","Tilting the head back","Washing the hands","Holding the eyes closed for 2 to 3 minutes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it is singled out as the most important step of all. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"At follow-up, a 61-year-old's documented visual acuity is worse than at the first visit. What does that require?","choices":["Repeating the course of drops","Reassurance and review in a week","Switching to an ointment","Immediate ophthalmology consult"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — which is the reason acuity is documented at every visit. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 33"}]};
